LegaCore CONSTRAINT DERIVATION revision 4, frozen.

CHANGE: one new resolution state, RESOLVED_ENCLOSING_SCOPE. It narrows NOTHING - a parameter is bound
for the whole body, so no boundary inside that body precedes its availability, and treating it as a
module binding would manufacture an ordering constraint from a name that was never unavailable.

EFFECT: the three B-SUPPORTED-INCOMPLETE cases are gone. Derived regions are IDENTICAL to revision 3
on all four families; only the account changed, which is the point.

STANDING: development. The three defects were found on the provenance family. The seven synthetic
witnesses in scope.test.mjs were written BEFORE the implementation and include four negatives - a
module-level name, a name bound nowhere, and both a parameter and a local for a module-level insertion
where neither is in scope. A resolver that answered enclosing_scope for everything fails all four.

KNOWN LIMIT, declared rather than discovered later: resolution is against the IMMEDIATE enclosing unit
only. A name reaching an operation through a closure over an outer function's locals stays UNRESOLVED,
which is the correct declared-gap behaviour rather than a silent wrong answer.

